00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.010
Anybody else?

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.810
How many of you are
engineers or people

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.570
who productionize models?

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:27.070
OK.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:28.250
A bit bigger group.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:28.970
Great.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:30.505
Thank you.

00:01:30.505 --> 00:01:32.630
I'm sure you've all had
different experiences doing

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:34.610
this but everybody has
probably faced this.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.109
You start with
thinking that you are

00:01:36.109 --> 00:01:38.240
going to be building a
model, and the moment you

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.670
start doing actual
work, you realize,

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.352
well, there is a
lot of other stuff

00:01:42.352 --> 00:01:44.060
you have to do around
before you can even

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.574
get your hands on TensorFlow or
something to train your model.

00:01:47.574 --> 00:01:49.490
You start with data and
you have to figure out

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:50.930
where to get data
into your system

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:55.880
from data analysis, data
transformation, validation.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.760
Actually, creating test
sets, training sets, then

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:02.480
training a model, building a
model, experiment a lot of it.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.520
And then you want to
validate your model

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.936
and figure out
whether it actually

00:02:05.936 --> 00:02:07.460
does what you are looking for.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.830
And after that, you
want to train at scale.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:11.210
Roll out the model production.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:12.320
If it doesn't go
into production,

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:13.600
I don't know why
it's [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.849
to train the model at all,
so I think that is probably

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:17.360
the most important step.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.430
And then, actually, solve
it and build probably

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:20.871
an application around it.

00:02:20.871 --> 00:02:22.370
Of course, then,
eventually you want

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.810
to monitor and have
logging, as well.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:31.780
So what's the problem in
this work flow, in the space?

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:36.172
Setting up an ML stack or a
pipeline is incredibly hard.

00:02:36.172 --> 00:02:37.630
Anybody who has
done this, I think,

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:40.270
would agree with me that when
you start doing the serialize,

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:41.940
you need to install
a lot of tools,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.440
either on your
desktop, workstation,

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.220
wherever you're working.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.531
You get subbed into
a lot of engineering

00:02:47.531 --> 00:02:49.780
and that [INAUDIBLE] where
you need to install and get

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.270
things connected, working
together, figure out networking

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:55.330
and I don't know what not.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:56.650
Talk to production engineers.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:01.024
And doing this on production
environment is even harder.

00:03:01.024 --> 00:03:03.190
You have to be careful,
you're not messing things up

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.550
that are already there.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.130
If you productionize your
model in the wrong way,

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.504
you might end up with making
completely wrong predictions

00:03:08.504 --> 00:03:10.900
from what the data scientist
intended when they actually

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.080
trained the model.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.840
And setting up an
ML stack that works

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.940
across different clouds
or different deployments

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.930
is extremely hard.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:25.380
In fact, I don't
know how if at all

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.690
is possible with the
current set of tools.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:32.260
And for our current purposes
for today's discussion,

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.350
we will consider your local
laptop on your desktop

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.490
to be multi-cloud, as well.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:40.341
It's just another
instance of the cloud.

00:03:40.341 --> 00:03:40.840
All right.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.180
If we look at our
experimentation stack,

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:44.830
when you are building
a model, you're

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:49.060
training locally, trying things
out, you have a lot of stuff

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.850
running on your laptop
and enabling you,

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:54.580
starting with hardware at
the bottom with accelerators,

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:58.640
operating system drivers,
runtime, and then storage.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:00.640
And on top of this, you
have your frameworks

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.848
that you actually use to
train and build your models.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.960
On top of the stack, on
your laptop or your desktop,

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.260
you're going to be installing
a bunch of micro services

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:13.140
that enable you to compose
and create your workflow

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.630
all the way from reading
and data, training,

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:19.899
and then testing out your
model and making predictions.

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:21.190
Now, we take it a step further.

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:24.110
You've got to do this
whole thing over again,

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.090
where you actually want to
train your model at scale.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.872
We all would love to train the
models with the volume of data

00:04:30.872 --> 00:04:31.830
that we have available.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.200
The more data
sets, the more data

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.910
points you are able to use.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:40.020
Hopefully, you get better
accuracy in your model.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.520
And then, finally,
you actually want

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.222
to deploy your model onto
cloud, where actually the end

00:04:44.222 --> 00:04:46.680
points are accessible to users
and they are actually making

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:48.580
a difference in the real world.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.230
The actual intention
of your model.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:52.970
For all three of
these environments,

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.242
unfortunately, right
now in the current set

00:04:56.242 --> 00:04:57.700
of technologies
that are available,

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.770
you have to literally
redo a lot of the stuff.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:02.522
What you did in your
experimentation stack

00:05:02.522 --> 00:05:03.980
to actually build
your model is not

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:07.160
exactly what you can take
to production or to deploy

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:08.580
on the cloud.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.480
And there's a ton of work
involved in making that happen,

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.270
which is why we thought
of building Kubeflow.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.420
And we started this effort.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.450
Our mission literally is to
make it easy for everyone

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.960
to develop, deploy, and
manage portable distributed

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.280
ML on Kubernetes.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:33.030
So in summary,
essentially, the platform

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.160
is going to allow
you to run Kubeflow

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.390
wherever you can run
Kubernetes on whatever cluster,

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:39.780
whatever cloud,
whether it's on prem

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.550
or some kind of cloud platform.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.610
If you can run Kubernetes on it,
you can deploy Kubeflow on it

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.780
and take your technology
stack along with it,

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:48.120
including your local machine.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.570
So you can run Kubernetes on
your local machines, as well.

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:53.970
With this technology,
essentially, this

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.470
is what your stack
should look like.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:59.010
You are literally working
with Kubeflow services,

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:00.837
and whatever you
deploy and develop,

00:06:00.837 --> 00:06:03.420
you should be able to translate
it across all the environments

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:04.253
without much effort.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.630
Looking at what's going
on inside Kubeflow,

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:12.690
we have a bunch
of micro services

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.870
that we rely on in
building our ML workflows.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:20.020
And we have integrated a
whole bunch of components,

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:23.780
some coming from Google itself,
from TensorFlow DFX and others

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:26.960
from open source community,
including things like TF

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:30.230
Transform, Numpy, Spark
for data transformations,

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:33.550
TF Data for data
validation, Jupiter

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.430
for actually a development
environment where

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:38.850
you experiment and build
your model, TF Job, MXNet,

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:42.220
PyTorch to actually
train your model at scale

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:46.400
and TF Serving, Seldom, TensorRT
for serving your model at scale

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.690
with the kind of
latencies you want

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:50.660
and Prometheus for monitoring.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:52.870
There is more stuff that
we can't fit on the slide,

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:55.270
but also, you will notice
there are boxes which

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:56.620
are right now missing contents.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:00.790
So we do need to build
out more competence

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:02.500
and get community
to get involved

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.500
and incorporate their
solutions into the platform.

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:12.240
All right, so that was
kind of the back story

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.260
on what Kubeflow
is about and how

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:17.110
it kind of lets you
translate your solutions

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:19.180
across different platforms.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:21.250
I want you to spend
some time on this slide

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.440
and think about, like,
what the user experience is

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:24.910
going to look like.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.804
People who have done
development of models,

00:07:27.804 --> 00:07:30.220
we want to kind of like see
what is the work flow you will

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:32.636
be going through when you are
trying to figure out how you

00:07:32.636 --> 00:07:34.900
develop and build your model.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:38.020
One of the important
goals of Kubeflow

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:44.170
is really creating a low bar
and high ceiling experience.

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:46.270
What do I mean by high ceiling?

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.760
If you notice the way
Kubeflow is designed

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:53.020
on top of Kubernetes, it does
not restrict any Kubernetes

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.420
level APIs.

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.770
And in every tool that
is available on Kubeflow,

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:59.890
you should be able to configure
to your heart's content

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:01.560
and in whatever
environment you are on.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:04.047
There is a lot of complexity
in Enterprise World,

00:08:04.047 --> 00:08:05.630
a lot of challenging
networking setup,

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:07.690
and different kind of
environments that you

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:09.450
might have to optimize for.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.950
And the configurations
in Kubeflow

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:14.380
should allow you to be
able to go down into detail

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:17.380
and modify whatever it
is that you need to make

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:18.860
it work in your environment.

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:21.580
But on the other hand,
you want to enable

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:24.790
users who are not familiar
with Kubernetes to be

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:27.700
able to access this platform
and be able to build Kubernetes

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:31.900
flows without having to worry
about learning Kubernetes.

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:33.970
Similarly on the
data science front

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:37.000
as well, I mean, there
are engineers who are just

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.470
starting learning data science.

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:41.110
They have a lot of good
background in Kubernetes

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:43.720
and systems, but they might
not be that familiar with data

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:44.220
science.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:46.540
We want to create a low
bar for them as well,

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:48.970
have tools that will
enable them to build data

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:50.650
science machine
learning products

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:52.630
without having to go
too much into detail

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:57.930
of learning various
technologies if possible.

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.950
OK, with that in
mind, I would like

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:01.900
to present an example
which will walk us

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:06.340
through the user experience
that we just talked about.

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:08.020
It's specifically focusing--

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:10.530
I think a lot of you are
already familiar with what

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:12.347
a GitHub issue is.

00:09:12.347 --> 00:09:13.930
Generally if you go
to GitHub, I think

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:17.590
there is a content associated
with the actual issue where

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:18.770
you have all the details.

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:20.260
And then you label--

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:23.230
your title the GitHub
issue with a title

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:25.360
that seems to fit or
describe, summarize,

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:26.832
what the issue is all about.

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:28.540
The problem we are
going to be looking at

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:31.990
is essentially a
user filing a bug.

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:34.180
And when they are
running into some issue,

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:36.254
they are not in
the right mindset,

00:09:36.254 --> 00:09:38.420
and they are really troubled
about what is going on.

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:41.260
And all they want is
to paste that error,

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:43.360
provide whatever information
you want to provide,

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:45.190
and then file the issue.

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:47.230
That might not be the
best time to think about

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:49.330
what exactly is going
on and what should

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:52.520
the real title for the bug be.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:55.580
So with that in mind, one
of the data scientists

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:58.720
at GitHub, Hamel, actually
thought about this problem

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:02.500
and felt that he can come up
with an ML algorithm or an ML

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:04.180
model that will
look at the content,

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:06.460
analyze it, and hopefully
suggest a title that

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:09.820
is relevant and helpful.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:12.400
So I want to thank
at this point Hamel

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.490
from GitHub, who actually
built this model,

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:17.290
and Ankush and
Michelle, who helped

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:21.880
production this into an
application on top of Kubeflow.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:25.090
And while I'm at the
stage of thanking people,

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:28.480
I should also thank a
whole lot of other people.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:32.100
None of the content that you're
seeing is actually my creation.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.930
It's a lot of people who have
actually helped create it.

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:37.030
And I personally really
want to thank Jeremy, who

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:39.190
