This is not directly
available so far.

00:11:38.437 --> 00:11:41.020
But I want to give you a preview
of what deployment experience

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:42.670
would look like with
this application

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:43.795
that we have been building.

00:11:43.795 --> 00:11:46.510
And this deployment
is a deployment

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:49.270
of Kubeflow on top of
Google Kubernetes engine.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:52.090
As I mentioned, you can deploy
Kubeflow wherever Kubernetes is

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:54.301
deployed, whether it's
Microsoft Azure, AWS,

00:11:54.301 --> 00:11:56.050
or some other
[? cloud-oriented ?] on prem

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:56.770
hardware.

00:11:56.770 --> 00:11:59.710
But we are specifically
focusing in this presentation

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:02.342
on a GKE-based deployment.

00:12:02.342 --> 00:12:04.300
You start with a few
[? set of ?] [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:07.120
You talk about providing
information about the Google

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:08.980
project, cloud
project that you want

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:13.690
to deploy the stack on, and
give the deployment a name,

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:16.630
and then provide some kind
of authorization information,

00:12:16.630 --> 00:12:18.520
and then be able to
say click deployment.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:22.900
And a few minutes
later, you should

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:24.340
be able to view
your cluster along

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:27.100
with the services running
on your Kubernetes engine

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:28.130
dashboard.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:29.200
So what this is showing?

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:31.144
Sorry about the small font.

00:12:31.144 --> 00:12:33.310
But essentially, these are
some of the core services

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:35.200
that Kubeflow deploys
on your cluster

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:36.580
when you create the deployment.

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.520
A lot of these are fundamental
to stitching together

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:44.090
your workflows or your process
to train or deploy a model.

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:46.630
The URL here that
you are looking at

00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:49.960
is essentially a way to
get into an access point

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:52.540
into your Kubeflow
cluster deployment.

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:55.380
Everything by default,
the way it is deployed

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:56.890
is protected in
your cloud project

00:12:56.890 --> 00:13:01.810
and not accessible from an
external IP with the exception

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:03.460
that we create this
ingress point which

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:06.460
has IP protection, Google
IP protection, that

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:09.010
allows you to access and
authorize any access that

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:10.351
is going into the cluster.

00:13:10.351 --> 00:13:12.100
So if you click on
that link, it takes you

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:13.240
to the Kubeflow dashboard.

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:15.100
Right now, it's very minimal.

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:16.630
And we are working
on figuring out

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:19.270
what is the right information
that should go in here

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:20.644
and what all makes sense.

00:13:20.644 --> 00:13:22.060
But at this point,
it kind of like

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:23.996
helps you bounce
off to a few things

00:13:23.996 --> 00:13:25.870
that you are most commonly
going to be doing.

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:27.703
The first thing that
it will help you get to

00:13:27.703 --> 00:13:30.070
is the JupyterHub, which is
where you will be actually

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:31.310
doing your development.

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:34.930
And that is the next step
in the deployment process.

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:38.800
I do want to take some time
here and look at referring back

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:42.430
to our principal that we talked
about of low bar, high ceiling.

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:46.840
We created the UI, web UI
driven deployment process,

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:48.910
to lower the bar on
deployment to make

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:50.950
it easy for data
scientists who are not

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:54.084
familiar with Kubernetes
to get on to Google Cloud

00:13:54.084 --> 00:13:55.500
and be able to
create the Kubeflow

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:57.580
cluster without much
effort, just a few clicks

00:13:57.580 --> 00:13:59.214
and a few keystrokes.

00:13:59.214 --> 00:14:00.880
But on the other hand,
we want to ensure

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:03.610
that anybody who's familiar
with what's going on

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:05.920
has complete control on
what their deployment is

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:07.160
going to look like.

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:10.150
So on that front, the
CLI based deployment

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:12.910
uses kftctl, which is
our command line tool.

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:15.460
And it lets you
control your deployment

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:17.050
with four sets of
commands-- init,

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:19.870
generate, apply, and delete.

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:23.200
We see an example of how that
workflow goes for deployment

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:24.440
on the right side.

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:27.340
So we start with initializing
your deployment and it ends up

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:30.940
like reading a few parameters,
environment variables,

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:33.435
determining what your
cloud configuration is

00:14:33.435 --> 00:14:35.560
going to look like, and
then creates an environment

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:36.060
variable.

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:37.930
And init's a directory
which is going

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:41.230
to be your Kubeflow application
referring to your Kubeflow

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:42.530
deployment.

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:44.350
Once that is done,
then you can--

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:46.060
and in this instance
again, it is

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:48.010
pointing to a GCP,
Google Cloud Platform.

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:51.020
So it will end up
creating a GKE cluster.

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:53.209
The next step is basically
generating the platform.

00:14:53.209 --> 00:14:55.000
And what that generate
does is it generates

00:14:55.000 --> 00:15:00.490
a lot of configuration about
what your GKE configuration is

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:02.020
going to look like.

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:05.440
The benefit of this is that that
generated set of configuration

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:07.840
is available locally from
where you are doing deployment,

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:10.152
and you have access
to that configuration.

00:15:10.152 --> 00:15:12.610
You can go and look in there
browse around what's going on,

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:15.070
and you are able to
tweak a lot of stuff

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:18.430
and modify things if you want
to create additional load pools,

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:20.872
you want to add [? GPUs ?]
right from the beginning.

00:15:20.872 --> 00:15:23.080
You can make a lot of
configuration changes in there,

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:26.260
whether you want to have
IEP or not, essentially.

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:29.410
And the next step is going to
be apply the platform, which

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:31.750
would actually take the
configuration that it generated

00:15:31.750 --> 00:15:33.940
in the previous step,
and then actually talk

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:36.670
to Google Deployment and
Cloud Deployment Manager

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:38.830
and create the GKE
cluster that was

00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:40.750
defined by the configuration.

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:44.056
The next step is going to
generate the k8s about--

00:15:44.056 --> 00:15:45.430
which are basically
configuration

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:48.580
for the various services that
are deployed on a GKE cluster.

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:51.370
So in one of the previous
slides when we looked at the GKE

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:54.220
cluster, it had a
list of services

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:56.980
that are running as
part of core Kubeflow.

00:15:56.980 --> 00:15:59.020
The configuration
for those services

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:00.610
is generated by this command.

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:03.130
And apply would again
go ahead and apply that.

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:05.290
But intermediates--
it allows you

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:06.820
to keep that
configuration available

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:09.890
and modify in whatever
form you feel is needed.

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:15.930
Switching back to
the Jupyter notebook,

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:17.549
you go to the JupyterHub.

00:16:17.549 --> 00:16:19.590
And this is the page you
land on which allows you

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:21.480
to create a Jupyter notebook.

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:23.730
If you notice here,
the first field in here

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:26.700
is letting you choose
a container image.

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:29.370
We have a few pre-built
container images.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:31.170
These are Docker
container images

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:35.850
which already have a TensorFlow
support for different versions

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:41.160
of TensorFlow along with the
images built for CPU or GPU.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:43.350
If you want, you can just
click Spawn at that point

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:45.390
and then say, like,
start my notebook.

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:48.960
But it also allows you to
configure further and specify

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:49.962
how many cores you want.

00:16:49.962 --> 00:16:52.170
So if you know your workload
is going to require GPU,

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:55.690
you can go ahead and specify
that you need GPU containers.

00:16:55.690 --> 00:16:58.664
And then you can specify how
much memory you might need.

00:16:58.664 --> 00:17:00.080
Once you've created
your notebook,

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:02.160
you are inside the notebook.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:05.700
And this is kind of your
playground where you go around.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:07.500
You can import
TensorFlow libraries.

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:09.930
If you are working
with some other Python

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:13.380
libraries, PyTorch, you should
be able to PIP install packages

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:14.619
here as well.

00:17:14.619 --> 00:17:16.599
And then you have access
to those packages,

00:17:16.599 --> 00:17:17.970
Python packages.

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:21.270
You have access to Pandas,
whatever other Python tools you

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:22.349
might want to use.

00:17:22.349 --> 00:17:24.359
This is where you will
spend a lot of time

00:17:24.359 --> 00:17:26.800
iterating through your model
and figuring out what works,

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:28.920
what doesn't work, and
how do you want your model

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:31.210
to look like connecting
to various data

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:33.861
and actually
training your model.

00:17:33.861 --> 00:17:35.610
Remember that at this
stage, you are still

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.430
running inside your container
that has got the Jupyter

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:39.880
notebook running.

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:42.749
So you're only working possibly
with a reasonably sized data

00:17:42.749 --> 00:17:44.790
set that will allow you
to iterate and experiment

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:47.050
on your model.

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:50.620
Once you have figured out what
your training script is going

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:53.080
to look like, the next
step in the process

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:56.130
is to build a Docker
container image.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:18:00.180
Currently, the process involves
defining a Docker file which

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:02.921
will specify what your
Docker container needs

00:18:02.921 --> 00:18:03.670
to have installed.

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:06.960
So for example, if you need to
install a few Python packages,

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:09.510
you will have corresponding
PIP installs in there.

00:18:09.510 --> 00:18:12.106
And then you copy in
your training script,

00:18:12.106 --> 00:18:14.230
Python transcript, that
you had from your notebook.

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:16.170
You can create a
corresponding Python file,

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:18.630
and you can include it
in your container image

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:21.150
along with any other supporting
libraries you might have

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:22.930
written that you want in there.

00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:25.417
And once you have
specified the Docker file,

00:18:25.417 --> 00:18:27.000
then you will use
Docker Build command

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:29.640
to build the Docker container
image and then G Cloud

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:32.950
to push it to Cloud from where
you can actually then use

00:18:32.950 --> 00:18:36.314
the container image to
create your training jobs.

00:18:36.314 --> 00:18:37.980
Another thing I do
want to call out here

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:42.630
is looking back at the
low bar, high ceiling.

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:45.210
I think for a few
data scientists,

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:48.480
this might be too much.

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:50.850
They are probably
capable of doing this.

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:54.670
But is this where they should
be spending most of their time?

00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:56.790
Which is why we are
thinking of integrating this

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.580
into a workflow that goes
directly from the notebook,

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:02.400
and you are able to take
your training exercise

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:04.890
and, from there
itself, click on Build.

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:07.350
And then at the back of
this, it will actually

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:09.070
create a container
image for you.

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:11.027
So there are different
ways we are thinking of

00:19:11.027 --> 00:19:13.110
based on the feedback we
are getting on how we can

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:16.910
lower the bar at every step.

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:18.956
All right, so let's
look at the next step,

00:19:18.956 --> 00:19:20.330
which is the most
exciting part--

00:19:20.330 --> 00:19:21.800
for me, at least--

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:24.230
is to take my training
script and actually run it

00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:26.710
on a distributed cluster,
creating a full scale

00:19:26.710 --> 00:19:29.860
TensorFlow model.

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:34.300
In any application that you
deploy in a Kubeflow cluster,

00:19:34.300 --> 00:19:35.780
the way it works is--

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:39.860
and I will go a bit more in
detail in a few more slides.

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:42.610
But essentially, it involves
generally two steps--

00:19:42.610 --> 00:19:45.790
creating a configuration
for your training job

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:49.690
or any other service that you
want to deploy on your cluster

00:19:49.690 --> 00:19:52.120
and then essentially
making sure that you

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:54.430
have that configuration's
parameters specified

00:19:54.430 --> 00:19:56.170
appropriately based
on what you need,

00:19:56.170 --> 00:19:58.685
like how many workers you
might need, where the model's

00:19:58.685 --> 00:20:01.060
input is going to come from,
where do you want the output

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:03.424
model to go, and
setting up sizes

00:20:03.424 --> 00:20:04.840
on what your
training set is going

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:08.080
to look like maybe possibly.

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:11.150
Once you have your
configuration ready,

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:13.600
then you can kick off your
training job by saying,

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:17.200
take this TF job configuration
that we just defined

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:20.014
and apply it on my
Kubeflow cluster.

00:20:20.014 --> 00:20:22.180
And that's the ks apply
command which actually takes

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:25.327
the configuration, then talks
to the cluster and says,

00:20:25.327 --> 00:20:26.410
bring up these containers.

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:27.970
Get it running.

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:31.210
And while that job
is running, you

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:33.430
would be able to use
various Kubeflow commands

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:35.680
to monitor the parts
that are running the job

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:38.800
to access the logs and
figure out what is going on

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:40.450
with the training job.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:41.770
Back to the same--

00:20:41.770 --> 00:20:43.780
I keep coming back
to the same theme,

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:47.470
but essentially, looking back
at low bar, high ceiling,

00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:51.460
we do want to allow data
scientists or anybody who

00:20:51.460 --> 00:20:54.280
actually just wants
a bit of flexibility

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:57.370
and ease to be able to go and
create their TensorFlow job

00:20:57.370 --> 00:20:59.830
configuration from a UI.

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:01.990
And this is really
possible because if we

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:03.850
look at the previous
slide, there

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:07.370
was a lot of configuration
which is defined for the job.

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:10.180
And we can literally interpret
and parse the underlying

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:13.600
configuration and generate
the UI corresponding

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:15.250
to that configuration
and allow you

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:17.410
to set the parameters
not from the command line

00:21:17.410 --> 00:21:19.030
but from the UI.

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:23.350
And here you should be able to
point to the container image

00:21:23.350 --> 00:21:26.230
that you want to use, how
many workers you want to use,

00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:30.200
and where to pull the
data from, et cetera.

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:34.250
Great-- once our model is
trained, the next step for us

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:37.480
is how do we solve this model.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:39.740
I believe the example
that I present

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:43.010
here is to kind of use
Tensorflow [? serving. ?]

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:45.180
And you could use
Selden as well,

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:46.910
which is available in Kubeflow.

00:21:46.910 --> 00:21:49.370
And we have a
GitHub Examples repo

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:54.440
that you can go and take a
look at for the GitHub issue

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:55.610
summarization example.

00:21:55.610 --> 00:21:57.500
And then you should be
able to look and see

00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:00.260
how the same thing can
be done using Selden.

00:22:00.260 --> 00:22:03.200
In this slide, I will walk
you through TensorFlow

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:06.680
[? Serving ?] to
serve the model.

00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:09.717
We talked about this
ksonnet that we use.

00:22:09.717 --> 00:22:11.300
I want to give you
a bit of background

00:22:11.300 --> 00:22:13.220
on what's going on over there.

00:22:13.220 --> 00:22:16.910
We use ksonnet, which is a
configuration language which

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:20.294
allows templating to describe
various configuration.

00:22:20.294 --> 00:22:21.710
And the power of
templating allows

00:22:21.710 --> 00:22:23.840
you to parametrize a
whole bunch of things

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:28.250
that you can specify and set
and modify your configuration

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:31.110
at the time of instantiation.

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:33.200
So all Kubeflow
components are described

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:36.140
by a corresponding Ksonnet
configuration, which

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:41.360
is available to you on your
configuration directory

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:44.330
that you created when you
were deploying the cluster.

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:47.210
From there, you should be
able to create services

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:51.860
by generating new service
components using the template

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:54.630
and then configure the
parameters as I'm doing here.

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:57.090
We generated a
ServeInception component,

00:22:57.090 --> 00:22:59.750
which is a brand new component
from the TF serving template.

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:01.940
And then we're setting
a few parameters here,

00:23:01.940 --> 00:23:05.470
talking about whether we want
an HTTP proxy, do we want to use

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:07.940
GPUs, where the
model is actually

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:09.982
going to be stored, where
to load the model from.

00:23:09.982 --> 00:23:11.689
And there are a bunch
of other parameters

00:23:11.689 --> 00:23:14.240
that I'm not using but you're
free to explore and figure out

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:16.580
what you want to configure.

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:19.510
And then the last step,
just like last time,

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:22.480
is once your configuration
is ready, you say ks apply,

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:24.880
and then it will
actually end up deploying

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:27.780
this component on the cluster
and bringing up your model.

00:23:30.870 --> 00:23:33.530
Finally, deploying a
web app around this

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:35.370
is a very similar process.

00:23:35.370 --> 00:23:39.140
You would end up probably using
Flake or some other Python

00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:40.940
library or whatever
is your favorite tool

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:45.980
to kind of build a web
app against the model

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:47.150
that we just created.

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:49.010
And once your
application is ready,

00:23:49.010 --> 00:23:51.710
you can create a corresponding
container image, deploy

00:23:51.710 --> 00:23:54.440
the container on
Kubeflow, and then you

00:23:54.440 --> 00:23:57.950
have your UI to your
model [? at ?] the back.

00:24:01.430 --> 00:24:04.320
I think I've been mentioning
this over and over,

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:06.200
but essentially, that
is the real theme

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:09.530
here is that we have been
working on making sure

00:24:09.530 --> 00:24:12.020
that we get the breadth
of the components that

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:14.900
are necessary across the whole
spectrum of the workflow all

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:18.050
the way from data ingestion
to deploying and running

00:24:18.050 --> 00:24:18.800
your applications.

00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:21.380
There is a lot of
work around this, more

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:23.540
than we can handle
by ourselves, which

00:24:23.540 --> 00:24:26.360
is why we are relying a lot
on our community to provide us

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:27.870
solutions as well.

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:30.380
And we would encourage you
to get involved as well.

00:24:30.380 --> 00:24:33.820
But while we work on this
breadth, we do study our users

00:24:33.820 --> 00:24:36.200
and get a lot of feedback
and figure out where

00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:37.490
they are having challenges.

00:24:37.490 --> 00:24:39.800
And we will leverage
that information

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:42.830
to drive lowering
the bar and improving

00:24:42.830 --> 00:24:45.920
the experience on
wherever we see there

00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:48.500
is pain in user experience.

00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:50.150
We have tremendous
momentum, I think,

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:54.240
since the project went
live early this year,

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:56.060
late last year, early this year.

00:24:56.060 --> 00:25:00.170
We have over 1,000 plus commits
now, 100 plus community members

00:25:00.170 --> 00:25:02.870
that are active, and
roughly 20 plus companies

00:25:02.870 --> 00:25:04.211
that are contributing.

00:25:07.510 --> 00:25:11.210
And you can get live stats
about this from our GitHub repo.

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:14.690
Finally, I do want to
mention Kubeflow is open.

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:16.200
We are an open community.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:20.180
We love people joining our
community and contributing.

00:25:20.180 --> 00:25:22.550
Open design, open
source, and we are

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:24.680
open to ideas,
which is why we are

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:28.040
hoping that more and more
data scientists get involved.

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:30.470
As I noticed today, there
are very few data scientists.

00:25:30.470 --> 00:25:33.770
I would love for you to go
to our website kubeflow.org,

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:36.360
go to our GitHub
repo, file issues.

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:37.790
Let's start there.

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:40.550
Why don't you file issues
on what is working or not

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:41.289
working for you?

00:25:41.289 --> 00:25:42.830
And then we'll get
you more involved.

00:25:42.830 --> 00:25:45.830
And hopefully, you will
have ideas to contribute.

00:25:45.830 --> 00:25:48.520
Happy to take some
questions if you guys have.

00:25:48.520 --> 00:25:49.770
AUDIENCE: Thank you, Abhishek.

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:51.260
One question I
had is earlier, we

00:25:51.260 --> 00:25:56.210
talked about different companies
creating their own ML pipelines

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:58.200
and things like that--

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:01.190
Uber's Michelangelo,
Facebook's FB Learner.

00:26:01.190 --> 00:26:03.810
How generalizable are
you guys designing this

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:06.980
and do you think that this, the
way you guys are designing it,

00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:10.700
could potentially apply
to use cases they have?

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:12.680
And how are you guys
kind of optimizing

00:26:12.680 --> 00:26:16.042
between being opinionated
and being more general?

00:26:16.042 --> 00:26:18.500
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Right, I think
we are starting from a place

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:20.180
where we are not opinionated.

00:26:20.180 --> 00:26:22.760
And we want to allow
the flexibility

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:25.790
and allow the community to
kind of come up with ideas

00:26:25.790 --> 00:26:26.724
and contribute.

00:26:26.724 --> 00:26:28.640
I think the biggest
challenge at the beginning

00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:30.890
is to kind of get the
breadth and make sure

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:33.644
that we have components, we have
support to cover the whole end

00:26:33.644 --> 00:26:34.310
to end workflow.

00:26:34.310 --> 00:26:36.530
I think one of the biggest
challenges if people are

00:26:36.530 --> 00:26:38.160
interested is data connectors.

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:40.580
This is a big pain point
for all enterprise solutions

00:26:40.580 --> 00:26:42.800
where people always
ask us, like,

00:26:42.800 --> 00:26:45.450
can we have connectors
to our data systems?

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:48.590
And similarly, I think you
mentioned Michelangelo,

00:26:48.590 --> 00:26:50.060
Facebook's FB Learner.

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:52.342
All of them have
various strengths.

00:26:52.342 --> 00:26:54.800
And I think we are welcoming
them to come and contribute it

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:57.870
to the [? OSS ?] community and
build it into the platform.

00:26:57.870 --> 00:26:59.450
We would love to have that.

00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:02.360
Not only those-- if anybody
else wants to build--

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:05.712
we have a lot of contributors
who have gone and, like, made

00:27:05.712 --> 00:27:07.170
the effort to take
their components

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:08.060
but make them available.

00:27:08.060 --> 00:27:09.976
And we would encourage
to continue to do that.

00:27:13.267 --> 00:27:15.960
AUDIENCE: Hey, thank
you for the talk.

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:19.290
So one of the issues
or one of the things

00:27:19.290 --> 00:27:21.600
that can help that
doesn't exist today

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:25.110
is to know what the
standard we are doing

00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:26.954
MLS because there is none.

00:27:26.954 --> 00:27:27.870
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Right.

00:27:27.870 --> 00:27:30.810
AUDIENCE: And how to do feature
engineering, how to do serving,

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:33.330
how to do training, and if
we are doing it in a way

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:36.600
that it is compliant
with the enterprises--

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:38.520
then being opinionated
then actually helps.

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:39.645
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Absolutely.

00:27:39.645 --> 00:27:41.259
AUDIENCE: So how
do you see this?

00:27:41.259 --> 00:27:43.050
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Right,
I'm not disagreeing.

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:44.549
I think your question
is very valid,

00:27:44.549 --> 00:27:46.929
and I do hear this from a
lot of customers we talk to.

00:27:46.929 --> 00:27:48.345
A lot of them are
at a place where

00:27:48.345 --> 00:27:50.010
they are tell me
one way to do this,

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:51.386
and that is what I want to do.

00:27:51.386 --> 00:27:53.010
I don't want to
explore and figure out.

00:27:53.010 --> 00:27:55.380
So we do see a
spectrum of people

00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:58.110
where there are really
sophisticated engineers

00:27:58.110 --> 00:28:00.679
and teams that are actually--
they want full control.

00:28:00.679 --> 00:28:02.220
But there are people
who are actually

00:28:02.220 --> 00:28:04.310
interested in, like, this
is my business problem.

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:06.310
That is what I want to
focus on and just tell me

00:28:06.310 --> 00:28:08.310
how to kind of like do this.

00:28:08.310 --> 00:28:12.510
I do see a time when being
opinionated would help.

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:14.970
But I feel like at the
beginning of the project,

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:17.290
it is not the right
time to form opinions

00:28:17.290 --> 00:28:20.520
where we won't even know
what the right opinions are.

00:28:20.520 --> 00:28:23.340
I think it is time for us
to kind of explore and allow

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:26.910
the community to contribute
ideas along with us producing

00:28:26.910 --> 00:28:28.300
ideas as well.

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:30.900
And hopefully, we find
out from our users,

00:28:30.900 --> 00:28:33.270
from customers,
what works for them.

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:36.460
We are willing to help them
set up when they are confused

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:38.522
or if they need some
handholding and guidance.

00:28:38.522 --> 00:28:39.980
We are willing to
put that forward.

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:44.370
Our whole community is happy to
do that in providing guidance.

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:46.860
But I think I don't
believe that--

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:48.630
if we start being
open ended right now,

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:51.150
I think we will end up
losing some good solutions,

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:53.190
and we might end up
creating a platform that

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:54.900
is not usable by all.

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:57.000
I think the whole ML
space, in my opinion,

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:58.700
is at a place where
as an industry,

00:28:58.700 --> 00:29:01.200
we don't really know what the
right way to do all the things

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:01.771
are.

00:29:01.771 --> 00:29:03.270
And we need to
figure that out first

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:06.690
before we can start imposing
those opinions on people.

00:29:09.902 --> 00:29:10.860
AUDIENCE: Good evening.

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:12.330
My name is Paul.

00:29:12.330 --> 00:29:14.390
Thank you for the information.

00:29:14.390 --> 00:29:17.130
It seems like a really great
tool to help a lot of people.

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:18.430
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Thank you.

00:29:18.430 --> 00:29:23.540
AUDIENCE: I've been hearing
a lot about KeyStores.

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:29.750
And I'm curious how Kubeflow
would play with like KeyStores

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:33.009
or managing identities.

00:29:33.009 --> 00:29:33.800
ABHISHEK GUPTA: OK.

00:29:33.800 --> 00:29:35.900
AUDIENCE: I know that
you have like an IAM

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:39.440
in the Google Cloud Platform.

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:40.535
Can you speak to that?

00:29:40.535 --> 00:29:41.910
ABHISHEK GUPTA:
Right, I think we

00:29:41.910 --> 00:29:47.270
are relying for identity and
access management to be--

00:29:47.270 --> 00:29:48.860
on the underlying
cloud platforms.

00:29:48.860 --> 00:29:51.230
For example, Kubeflow
itself is not

00:29:51.230 --> 00:29:53.510
reinventing the wheel on
a lot of underlying cloud

00:29:53.510 --> 00:29:54.500
infrastructure.

00:29:54.500 --> 00:29:57.440
So on Google Cloud, we
rely completely on--

00:29:57.440 --> 00:29:59.900
in fact, we will rely
heavily on Kubernetes

00:29:59.900 --> 00:30:03.764
to design and define those
solutions and leverage that.

00:30:03.764 --> 00:30:05.430
And in addition to
that, when you deploy

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:08.570
Kubernetes on different
cloud providers,

00:30:08.570 --> 00:30:11.210
we will be leveraging
the IAM solutions that

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:14.226
might be available over there.

00:30:14.226 --> 00:30:15.100
Does that make sense?

00:30:15.100 --> 00:30:16.141
AUDIENCE: Yes, thank you.

00:30:16.141 --> 00:30:18.379
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Thank you, Paul.

00:30:18.379 --> 00:30:18.920
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:30:18.920 --> 00:30:22.220
I was wondering if you could
help us position Kubeflow

00:30:22.220 --> 00:30:25.820
against some of those managed
machine learning platforms

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:28.144
and how you see it from
the Google perspective.

00:30:28.144 --> 00:30:29.060
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Right.

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:31.970
I think it will be a
good idea to do that.

00:30:31.970 --> 00:30:35.330
There are definitely users,
as we just discussed,

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:37.830
who can benefit from
managed services as well.

00:30:37.830 --> 00:30:40.160
And I think it's an important
part of their experience

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:42.590
where they are willing
to experiment and go deep

00:30:42.590 --> 00:30:43.790
into certain areas.

00:30:43.790 --> 00:30:45.560
But there are portions
of the ML workflow

00:30:45.560 --> 00:30:47.342
where they would
not want to invest.

00:30:47.342 --> 00:30:48.800
And it would be
great to integrate.

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:51.980
I think that also ties into the
question that Mark, I think,

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:56.780
had asked about how do we get
FB Learner or, for example,

00:30:56.780 --> 00:31:00.559
Michelangelo or maybe Google
Cloud's manage offerings

00:31:00.559 --> 00:31:01.850
to integrate into the platform.

00:31:01.850 --> 00:31:03.920
I think it is
important for us for us

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:06.560
to figure out integration
points with those services,

00:31:06.560 --> 00:31:07.650
for example.

00:31:07.650 --> 00:31:09.350
People might be
willing to spend time

00:31:09.350 --> 00:31:13.100
and are OK with things failing
or experimentation going on

00:31:13.100 --> 00:31:14.210
in training phase.

00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:17.100
But they want to leverage the
strength of Google's [? CLE ?]

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:18.470
platform for serving.

00:31:18.470 --> 00:31:20.780
Or similarly, there could
be other managed services

00:31:20.780 --> 00:31:21.780
that might be providing.

00:31:21.780 --> 00:31:26.326
So I think that's an interesting
idea and very useful as well.

00:31:26.326 --> 00:31:28.700
We should definitely look at
all those solutions as well.

00:31:28.700 --> 00:31:31.039
I would not discount
any of those.

00:31:31.039 --> 00:31:32.330
Does that answer your question?

00:31:32.330 --> 00:31:35.410
Cool, thanks.

00:31:35.410 --> 00:31:38.710
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] you
mentioned at the beginning

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:42.760
that [INAUDIBLE]
Kubeflow [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:42.760 --> 00:31:46.990
deploy not only on the cloud
but also on the other clusters.

00:31:46.990 --> 00:31:49.054
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Right.

00:31:49.054 --> 00:31:52.180
AUDIENCE: Can you currently
deal with other kind of cluster

00:31:52.180 --> 00:31:53.754
systems like HPC systems?

00:31:53.754 --> 00:31:55.670
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Right,
that's a good question.

00:31:55.670 --> 00:31:58.120
So I think, I mean, we
rely heavily on Kubernetes

00:31:58.120 --> 00:32:01.810
to provide us the
capabilities on deploying

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:03.220
on different platforms.

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:06.454
And on prem is, for
example, one such platform

00:32:06.454 --> 00:32:08.370
where we are working
with certain partners who

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:11.530
are focusing on making
this work to work on

00:32:11.530 --> 00:32:13.670
like high performance
clusters, for example,

00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:15.787
if there are InfiniBand
connects or if there

00:32:15.787 --> 00:32:18.120
are other kind of interconnects
that, for example, Cisco

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:20.337
has some high bandwidth
connections as well.

00:32:20.337 --> 00:32:22.420
So we would rely on some
of our community partners

00:32:22.420 --> 00:32:27.122
to help us port these platforms
onto those hardware platforms.

00:32:27.122 --> 00:32:29.080
AUDIENCE: This is currently
still not available

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:29.950
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:32:29.950 --> 00:32:30.866
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Right.

00:32:30.866 --> 00:32:33.820
I am not aware of this, but
I do know that, for example,

00:32:33.820 --> 00:32:38.080
certain of partners are
working on the solution.

00:32:38.080 --> 00:32:39.990
Right, but this is
certainly something

00:32:39.990 --> 00:32:41.900
that we are interested in.

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:43.249
Thank you.

00:32:43.249 --> 00:32:44.540
PRESENTER: Thanks, [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:44.540 --> 00:32:45.623
ABHISHEK GUPTA: Thank you.

00:32:45.623 --> 00:32:47.250
Thank you, guys.

